---
// A short introduction to Chapter 2 of the dissertation, the chapter that holds Studies 1 and 2.
import { href } from '../utils/url';
interface Props { current?: '1' | '2' }
const { current } = Astro.props;
---
<aside class="chapter" aria-labelledby="chapter-title">
  <h2 id="chapter-title">Chapter 2: Is the gap real, and what is behind it?</h2>
  <p>
    The first chapter of the experiments asks a plain question. When a doctor and a medical AI are judged on the same
    health decision, do people trust the doctor more, and is that true for every kind of decision? Both studies take the
    view of an ordinary patient, using broad medical decisions rather than one specific scenario.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chapter also tests a first explanation. Clinical expertise blends quantitative reasoning (data and statistics) with
    intuition (experience and judgment), and an AI is usually thought to have only the first. If people trust doctors more
    because they credit them with both, ratings of these two abilities should track trust.
  </p>
  <p>
    {current === '1' ? <strong>Study 1</strong> : <a href={href('/projects/trust-in-medical-ai/study-1/')}>Study 1</a>} (188 adults) rated fifteen decisions and
    eleven recommenders, from a doctor to an AI fitness coach.
    {current === '2' ? <strong>Study 2</strong> : <a href={href('/projects/trust-in-medical-ai/study-2/')}>Study 2</a>} (106 adults) replicated the gap on twelve
    concrete decisions, rated a doctor and an AI side by side, and added warmth, competence and mind perception. Together they set up Chapters 3 and 4,
    where I try to close the gap by making an AI seem more human.
  </p>
</aside>
<style>
  .chapter { margin: 2rem 0 0; padding: 1.1rem 1.4rem 0.4rem; border-left: 4px solid var(--doctor); background: var(--wash); max-width: 70ch; }
  .chapter h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 0 0 0.6rem; }
  .chapter p { margin: 0 0 0.8rem; }
</style>
